# oe-archive.crontab — THE crontab of abhinav@192.168.100.252, repo-managed since 2026-08-15.
# Installed and verified ONLY by Jenkins archive-scripts-deploy (crontab <this file>, then a
# byte-for-byte readback diff). Hand edits are drift: the next deploy refuses until reconciled.
@reboot /usr/bin/sg input -c /home/abhinav/disk_space_beep.sh >/tmp/disk_space_beep.log 2>&1
17 * * * * /home/abhinav/oe-ops/ibkr-raw-retention.sh

# --- daily training-data archive -------------------------------------------------------------
# CRON_TZ applies to the entries BELOW it. The box runs Europe/Madrid; pinning the schedule to
# New York keeps this at 17:10 ET (market close + 1h10m) across BOTH daylight-saving switches.
# Converting to local time and hardcoding it would drift an hour, twice a year, in opposite
# directions. The script itself skips market holidays via market_calendar.py.
# CRON_TZ works here because this host's cron is cronie (CentOS Stream 9, cronie-1.5.7-8.el9, checked
# 2026-09-11), which schedules every entry below this line in New York time. Not every cron does: a
# cron without CRON_TZ support would run all of them at Madrid time, six hours early. Install this file
# only on a cronie host, or convert the schedule first. test-archive-reset.sh §15 asserts that every ET
# entry sits below this line.
CRON_TZ=America/New_York
10 17 * * 1-5 /home/abhinav/oe-ops/oe-archive-daily.sh >> /home/abhinav/oe-ops/archive-daily.cron.log 2>&1
# Spot ticks must be archived FAR more often than once a day. underlying.spx.price is
# cleanup.policy=compact,delete with min.cleanable.dirty.ratio=0.01 and segment.ms=1h, so a
# closed segment is compacted almost at once: on 2026-08-03 only 3,306 of 804,629 offsets were
# still readable (99.6% gone) and the daily 17:10 run captured 9,464. Only the ACTIVE segment is
# safe from the cleaner, so archiving every 10 min captures the ticks while they are still there.
# Cheap and incremental — a no-op run costs nothing because offsets are checkpointed.
#
# ARCHIVE_JOB=spot is load-bearing, not a label. Until 2026-08-12 this entry and the 17:10 daily
# run shared ONE lock keyed only on (env, destination); this job held it at 17:10 and the daily job
# exited 0 having archived nothing, every weekday from 08-10. The job name is half the lock key now,
# and the topic list here must stay DISJOINT from OE_HEAVY_TOPICS_prod in oe-topics.env — the list
# below is exactly OE_SPOT_TOPICS.
*/10 * * * * TOPICS="underlying.spx.price underlying.spx.index.price underlying.es.price spx.basis.state" ARCHIVE_DIR=/mnt/nas/optionsedge ENV=prod ARCHIVE_JOB=spot JAVA_HOME=$(ls -d /usr/lib/jvm/*jre-17* 2>/dev/null|head -1) /home/abhinav/oe-ops/oe-archive-kafka.sh >> /home/abhinav/oe-ops/archive-spot.log 2>&1
# dev dealer-ledger evidence every 2h: dev signal-fired has a 24h retention the service re-stamps,
# so a once-daily capture is one missed run from losing a session permanently. 2026-08-08.
5 */2 * * * /home/abhinav/oe-ops/oe-archive-dev.sh >> /home/abhinav/oe-ops/archive-dev.cron.log 2>&1
# es4 tape at 17:01 ET, IMMEDIATELY at the ES close: the session is prev-day 18:00 -> 17:00,
# and the market reopens at 18:00 — capture must land in the 17:00-18:00 break so the dt=<date>
# folder holds exactly one session, never the next session's first records. (A 2h cron was
# tried 2026-08-08 and replaced same day: its post-close runs would mix the reopened session
# into the same dt folder.) es.underlying.es.trades has 24h retention and was archived nowhere
# — Friday 08-07 tape expired mid-study and was re-pulled from Databento at cost. The 17:10
# nightly wrapper archives the same topics into the same manifest as a backstop (a no-op when
# this run succeeded). Sunday-evening records are captured by Monday's run, still inside 24h.
#
# NO TOPICS override here, deliberately (2026-08-15): the es4 set is OE_ES4_TOPICS in
# oe-topics.env — one definition, every caller — and the archiver REQUIRES it. A list pasted
# here is how es.futures.cvd.bars went unarchived while three gates shipped against it.
1 17 * * 1-5 ARCHIVE_DIR=/mnt/nas/optionsedge ENV=es4 ARCHIVE_JOB=es4 BOOTSTRAP=192.168.100.4:9092 JAVA_HOME=$(ls -d /usr/lib/jvm/*jre-17* 2>/dev/null|head -1) /home/abhinav/oe-ops/oe-archive-kafka.sh >> /home/abhinav/oe-ops/archive-es4.log 2>&1
# Completeness check — THE safety net, and deliberately its own cron entry rather than a step at
# the end of the nightly job. The 2026-08-10..08-11 loss was invisible precisely because every
# piece of evidence came from the job that was failing: its exit status, its log, its own
# self-report. This entry reads the files that are actually on the NAS and alerts on a missing or
# partial date whether or not the 17:10 job ran, succeeded, or existed at all. If you ever find
# yourself moving this inside oe-archive-daily.sh, re-read this paragraph.
#
# 20:00 ET, i.e. 2h50m after the nightly job starts. That margin is not padding: measured on
# 2026-08-11, options.databento.gex.strike alone costs ~33 min because it is compact,delete and the
# consumer therefore waits out its full 60s idle timeout on each of 33 partitions. A verifier that
# fires while the job it checks is still writing reports PARTIAL and pages someone for nothing, and
# an alarm people learn to ignore is worse than no alarm. If the nightly run ever grows past this
# margin, move THIS entry later — do not shorten the consumer timeout, because an early timeout is
# indistinguishable from a clean read and would checkpoint over a short read silently.
0 20 * * 1-5 ENV=prod ARCHIVE_DIR=/mnt/nas/optionsedge /home/abhinav/oe-ops/oe-archive-verify.sh >> /home/abhinav/oe-ops/archive-verify.cron.log 2>&1
# es4 completeness, 20:05 ET — its own entry for the reason given above: it reads what is on the NAS,
# not what the 17:01 es4 job said about itself. Until 2026-09-11 nothing verified es4 at all: the
# floors in oe-topics.env (the strike presence floor among them) were declared and never checked,
# because the verifier requires OE_ALL_TOPICS_es4 and it did not exist (deploy Codex final review,
# finding 3). The verifier delivers its own alert on MISSING / PARTIAL / CORRUPT or on being unable to
# run, exactly as the prod entry does. Five minutes after prod so the two runs never interleave.
5 20 * * 1-5 ENV=es4 ARCHIVE_DIR=/mnt/nas/optionsedge LOG=/home/abhinav/oe-ops/archive-verify-es4.log /home/abhinav/oe-ops/oe-archive-verify.sh >> /home/abhinav/oe-ops/archive-verify-es4.cron.log 2>&1

# --- A5: the calibration corpus ------------------------------------------------------------------
# SECOND archive pass, 20:45 ET. The 17:10 run can legitimately precede the session seal: a call
# drained late (the ES stream lagged) finalises its horizons after the close, and the seal waits for
# every one of them. Re-reading the archive cannot discover a seal that is still sitting in Kafka —
# only another archive pass can. Without this the corpus would carry sessions that are permanently
# PENDING_SEAL, which the reader must treat as incomplete.
45 20 * * 1-5 ENV=prod ARCHIVE_DIR=/mnt/nas/optionsedge ARCHIVE_JOB=seal ON_LOCK_BUSY=fail TOPICS=context-tape.direction.ledger JAVA_HOME=$(ls -d /usr/lib/jvm/*jre-17* 2>/dev/null|head -1) /home/abhinav/oe-ops/oe-archive-kafka.sh >> /home/abhinav/oe-ops/archive-seal.cron.log 2>&1

# Calibration progress, 21:00 ET — AFTER the second archive pass, so it reads a corpus that can
# actually contain today's seal. It answers one question and writes it where a human can read it:
# how far is calibration, and did today land at all. A silent reporter and an empty corpus look the
# same from the outside, so a separate watchdog on a different host asserts this ran.
# BOOTSTRAP is set because the record is DELIVERED as well as written: A5.7 publishes the same record to
# context-tape.direction.progress, which is the panel's only way to see it. With BOOTSTRAP unset the run
# would write the NAS copy and say so, and a reader could mistake that silence for a successful publish.
30,40,50 20 * * 1-5 ENV=prod ARCHIVE_DIR=/mnt/nas/optionsedge BOOTSTRAP=localhost:9092 /home/abhinav/oe-ops/oe-calibration-progress.sh >> /home/abhinav/oe-ops/calibration-progress.cron.log 2>&1
0 21 * * 1-5 ENV=prod ARCHIVE_DIR=/mnt/nas/optionsedge BOOTSTRAP=localhost:9092 /home/abhinav/oe-ops/oe-calibration-progress.sh >> /home/abhinav/oe-ops/calibration-progress.cron.log 2>&1
# DEV, at 21:05 — after the 2-hourly dev archive has had its 20:05 run. calibration-targets.env
# declares a dev cohort and the gateway relays a dev progress record; without this entry both were
# describing something nobody ever produced (r12 #4). No BOOTSTRAP: the dev broker is not on this host,
# so the record is written to the NAS only, and the run says so rather than implying it published.
5 21 * * 1-5 ENV=dev ARCHIVE_DIR=/mnt/nas/optionsedge /home/abhinav/oe-ops/oe-calibration-progress.sh >> /home/abhinav/oe-ops/calibration-progress-dev.cron.log 2>&1
#
# The WATCHDOG is deliberately NOT here. It must run on a different host and a different schedule
# than the reporter, or it shares the reporter's failure: a box that is down reports nothing and
# also fails to notice that nothing was reported. It is installed on the dev Mac (launchd,
# 07:00 ET) as calibration-progress-watch.sh, checking the PREVIOUS trading day.
#
# The A5.8 EVALUATOR is deliberately not here either, and for a different reason than the watchdog.
# oe-push-validation-artifact.sh is OFFLINE by design: it evaluates ONE pinned corpusVersion against a
# PREREGISTERED stopping boundary. A nightly run would produce a new artifact every evening over a
# growing corpus, and the first evening whose numbers happened to clear the floors would be the one
# anybody quoted — which is choosing the stopping point after seeing the data, the exact thing the
# preregistration exists to forbid. It is run by hand, once, when the boundary is reached:
#   PARAMETER_SET_HASH=... TRACK_FROM_PUSH=... STOPPING_BOUNDARY_MS=... CORPUS_VERSION=... \
#   ENV=prod ARCHIVE_DIR=/mnt/nas/optionsedge /home/abhinav/oe-ops/oe-push-validation-artifact.sh
